Under SPA Section 1.1, Buyer acquires 100% of the Company's stock. That transfers far more than 50% of the voting equity, so it triggers Pawford's termination right unless Pawford consents in advance. Pawford is the Company's largest customer, at about $31.6M a year. Schedule 4.6 nonetheless lists no third-party consents. As a result, three protections that run in Buyer's favor and turn on that list do not cover the Pawford consent:
- Seller's covenant to seek consents (Section 6.3);
- the closing delivery of consents (Section 3.1(f));
- Buyer's closing condition (Section 7.2(c)).

The schedule entry also carves the Pawford conflict out of the no-conflicts representation. Section 4.5(c) excepts matters "as set forth on Schedule 4.6," and Section 4.6 is likewise qualified by the schedule. This weakens Buyer's indemnity position for the conflict. Any recovery would also be capped and backed by an escrow that lasts only six months.

The harm falls on Buyer, the firm's client. After Closing, Buyer would own a company whose key customer can walk away on 60 days' notice. Nothing in the folder cures this, waives it, or records Buyer accepting it. The latest date in the folder is the March 4, 2026 signing, and no Closing appears to have occurred. Buyer can therefore still act: it can require that Schedule 4.6 be corrected, that Pawford's consent be added as a condition, or that it get a specific indemnity before Closing.
